00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
Or less.

182
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
There's a key element of being a scouter-

183
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Whether you're a Scout Master or Assistant Scout Master, you're on the troop committee or something like that,

184
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,000
And that is making judgments and making decisions.

185
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:01,000
This may be oversimplifying the case for some decisions and judgments, but let's divide them into two groups.

186
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:05,000
One is objective and one is subjective.

187
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:10,000
Objective things are easy to decide and to judge.

188
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:22,000
For instance, if a Scout comes to you to have a requirement signed off and that requirement is that he swam for a hundred yards,

189
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
that's a pretty objective judgment, right there,

190
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Diddy, or didn't he,

191
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:37,000
If you're a Merripadge counselor and a Scout, supposed to camp out for X number of nights to qualify for a requirement for the Merripadge?

192
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
objective judgment-

193
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Very simple,

194
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:48,000
And there's just not a whole lot of question about that where we have to try and sort out different issues and things.

195
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Somebody suggests that, hey, let's take the troop and go play paintball.

196
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:58,000
And you go to the guide to save scouting and you see that it is specifically prohibited

197
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
And that's an objective judgment.

198
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,000
It's pretty easy.

199
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:07,000
An assistant scout master asks if he can sit on a board of review for a rank.

200
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:12,000
You go to the guide to advancement 2011,. objective judgment:

201
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,000
It says, right there, no, assistant scout masters don't do this.

202
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
So there are matters of policy.

203
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:23,000
There are matters in the advancement system where there are clear, objective judgments.

204
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:30,000
The other kind of judgment, that subjective judgment, let's say, in advancement, did a scout show scout spirit?

205
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,000
Was he active in his troop and patrol.

206
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:38,000
Okay, that's a subjective judgment because there's no clearly defined objective standard

207
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:45,000
And those subjective judgments seem to be where we really run into a lot of problems.

208
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:55,000
I get a lot of emails about scout masters or other scouters who are kind of stuck on having to make these subjective judgments.

209
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:59,000
And then there's the question of who gets to make this decision.

210
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Do the youth leaders get to make this decision,

211
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Or does this scout master make this decision,

212
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
Or does the committee make the decision?

213
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:16,000
And pretty soon you've opened up a can of beans or you're in a real kettle of fish or any other kind of food stuff of your choice

214
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:20,000
And things come to a grinding halt and feelings get involved in it

215
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
And these things can really blow up

216
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:29,000
And they can make life very difficult for us as scouters and it can make life difficult for our scouts and their families.

217
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:38,000
So let's talk about a method of arriving at a subjective judgment or decision,

218
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:46,000
And the method I want to suggest to you is not to make this judgment or the decision based on what you think and your opinion.

219
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:50,000
We have to have a pretty strong personality to be a scouter,

220
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:55,000
Especially to be a cub master or scout master or a den leader or something like that.

221
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:01,000
It's good to have a pretty strong personality and to be grounded in your opinions and things like that.

222
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:08,000
But it can also be a double edged sword, because when you get into these areas of subjective judgment,

223
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:11,000
you think, oh man, this is something that I have to decide.

224
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:16,000
And you know, if you're a scouter, you know normally you have a pretty big heart

225
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and you don't want to make the wrong decision,

226
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
You don't want to arrive at the wrong judgment,

227
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
And it can be really really upsetting.

228
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:29,000
So for me, there's a surefire way to do this,

229
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
And it's very, very simple,

230
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:38,000
And that is is don't rely on your own opinion and don't rely on your own judgment,

231
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
Not exclusively.

232
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
So whose opinion and whose judgment do we rely on?

233
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,000
If we're dealing with a broader question of program or something like that,

234
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:52,000
we can rely on the collective wisdom of the Patrol Leaders Council.

235
00:17:52,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Now, some of you are going to say that a phrase like the collective wisdom of the Patrol Leaders Council

236
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
is a contradiction in terms,

237
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:03,000
But you know what

238
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
They know more than you think that they do.

239
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,000
If it's dealing with one specific scout,

240
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:17,000
we're going to rely on the judgment and the opinion of that scout.

241
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
I'll tell you how to do it in a moment,

242
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:24,000
But first let me tell you why it would be important to do this.

243
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,000
What is our goal?

244
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Our main aim in scouting is to develop happy, useful, contributing members of society.

245
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:36,000
We can all agree on that point, right.

246
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,000
To be a happy, useful, contributing member of society, you need an internal standard.

247
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:47,000
You need an internal standard that's informed by a moral code and an ethical code.

248
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:51,000
We have a moral and ethical code called the Scout Oath and the Scout Law,

249
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:59,000
And what we want to see- our main aim- is to see our Scouts weave the moral and ethical code

250
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:03,000
of the Scout Oath and the Scout Law into their characters

251
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:08,000
And to be able to call on that as something that motivates them in life.

252
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:12,000
that's something that illuminates their judgments and their decisions.

253
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:18,000
So for that to happen, they've got to have some practice at making judgments and decisions.

254
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:21,000
They need to develop that internal standard and call on it.

255
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:28,000
When we call on a scout's internal standard to judge for themselves,

256
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:33,000
we're strengthening the idea that how others see them is not as important

257
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
as what motivates them internally.

258
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:42,000
We want them to judge their own actions based on self-knowledge and self-motivation.

259
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:46,000
We want them to understand that their needs and interests are only going to be met

260
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:49,000
in the context of a wider community,

261
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:54,000
And making this happen is at the very heart of our work as Scouters.

262
00:19:54,000 --> 00:20:01,000
So that's why I think this method of making these subjective judgments is important.

263
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:02,000
So how do you do it?

264
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:07,000
Well, if you're a long-time listener to the podcast or reader of the blog,

265
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:11,000
this is not going to be a surprise to you. but you ask questions,

266
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:18,000
Because every time you ask a question, what we're doing is we're getting a scout to look inward

267
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
and to find an answer there.

268
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:23,000
So here's a couple of for instances. okay,

269
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:27,000
And these are actually based in a couple of emails that I've received.

270
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:33,000
Let's say that we have an older scout who has a bit of a checkered career in his attitude

271
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:37,000
and the way that he does things and just kind of the way he comes off to people.

272
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:44,000
And he comes to us seeking appointment to being like a junior assistant scout master. let's say:

273
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:47,000
And man, we've watched this boy,

274
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:55,000
Even though he's managed to become an Eagle Scout, even though he served as a leader in the troop in the past,

275
00:20:55,000 --> 00:21:03,000
he's got an attitude and he's had to apologize a number of times, and you know, I think you get the idea right.

276
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
So how do we deal with this?

277
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:13,000
If it's up to us to make a judgment whether or not he's qualified for that particular appointment

278
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:18,000
and we just exercise our judgment or our opinion unilaterally,

279
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:23,000
what we're doing is we're denying this scout an incredible growth opportunity,

280
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:28,000
looking at his own internal standard and seeing if he meets that standard.

281
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:32,000
To get him to do that, we sit down and we ask him some questions.

282
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:39,000
Maybe this particular scout needs to think about the relationship between power and authority

283
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:44,000
and rank and service and responsibility.

284
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Maybe he needs to be asked some questions about his past behavior, not in a judgmental way.

285
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:59,000
Maybe he needs to be asked to explain how that jibes with his internal standard of how people ought to be.

286
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:07,000
So by inviting that judgment, through asking questions, we help him strengthen that internal standard.

287
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:11,000
I mean, the alternative way of doing this is just to take the request

288
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:14,000
please appoint me to be a junior assistant scout master and just saying

289
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:21,000
nope, ain't going to do it because you've got an attitude problem and I don't want to deal with it.

290
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:25,000
Or we can see if we can actually advance the potential of this scout

291
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:28,000
and the incredibly strong purposes and aims of scouting

292
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:33,000
By working with him to find the answer to that question.

293
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:37,000
You know, a common thing that happens at a scout master's conference

294
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:44,000
is you're going to probably sign off the requirement for scout spirit or active participation

295
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:48,000
or the fulfillment of a position of responsibility, and those are subjective things

296
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:56,000
And we talked a little bit about this back when we were talking about advancement a couple of podcasts ago.

297
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:01,000
The way to make that judgment is not to make that judgment for yourself,

298
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:05,000
but to bring the scouts internal standard into play.

299
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:11,000
Another common difficulty is questions of discipline or responsibility or accountability.

300
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
Don't make those decisions on your own.

301
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:16,000
Don't just issue an opinion.

302
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:20,000
Tap the scouts inner standard, not just making a bunch of statements,

303
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:24,000
but by asking questions and really really listening to the replies

304
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:31,000
and work right along with them to come up and craft a solution to whatever problem has arisen.

305
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
And in these questions of discipline and accountability and things like that,

306
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:38,000
that's why I'm not a big fan of creating a whole bunch of rules.

307
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:42,000
We have the scout oath and we have the scout law. that's the rule.

308
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:43,000
Those are the rules.

309
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
How has that scout interpreted them?

310
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:51,000
How does he justify his actions by the scout oath and law?

311
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
Or how does the scout oath and law condemn his actions.

312
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:58,000
It's an independent, it becomes an independent moral and ethical force

313
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:03,000
rather than just the scout master's judgment of right and wrong.

314
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:08,000
And when you set this up as this independent moral and ethical force,

315
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:12,000
then both you and the scout are on the same side of the table, aren't you?

316
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
And you're looking at it and you're saying,

317
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
okay, you know, we had a bit of a problem here,

318
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
So let's talk about a little bit.

319
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
Let me ask you a few questions about it.

320
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:25,000
So there are objective questions of policy or advancement metrics

321
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:26,000
or things like that,

322
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,000
And those objective questions they're pretty easy to decide.

323
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:33,000
But as a scout leader, as a scout master, cub master, den leader, whatever

324
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:37,000
you are going to be in the position of making these subjective judgments,

325
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,000
My advice: don't make them on your own.

326
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:42,000
By asking questions and listening carefully,

327
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:48,000
tap a scout's own internal standard of the scout oath and law

328
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:53,000
and arrive at an answer to that subjective judgment together.

329
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:14,000
Well, thanks for listening to this edition of the Scout Master Podcast.

